>
>   Don't forget that body armor is just one of shirt, body, cloak, boots,
> helm, gloves, and shield.  Enough accessory armor can easily make up for
> the two points of AC difference between elven mithril and studded
> leather armor.  (So can a single highly enchanted piece of accessory
> armor.)

You can't "make up for" AC difference. Your AC can always be better. This
logic makes sense if your target AC is 0 or even -10. My target AC
is -100.

>   You both seem to be over-focussed on a single type of a single form of
> AC.  (Other forms include: divine protection, spell of protection, rings
> of protection, etc.)

In a discussion about which body armor to choose, focusing on body armor
seems, well, reasonable. :) You're right that there are many other ways to
get two or three points of AC. My counterpoint would be that these ways stack,
and I am more likely to run into body armor first. It is good that you
bring up the big picture, though (see below)...

>   A big problem is that the "best" wizard body armor changes rapidly in
> the very early game.  A wizard should probably wear the first non-cursed
> body armor that doesn't cause burdening, whether that's a leather jacket
> or bronze plate mail.   (That can bring up stashing strategies.)

Agreed.

>   After that almost any non-cursed armor still needs to be tried on to
> determine enchantment.  If you find +5 orcish ring mail, wear it.

But what if you have +5 elven mithril and +5 studded leather? Then the
debate goes back to "In the early game, are 3 points of AC worth a hit to
spellcasting ability?" Of course this is a theoretical discussion and a
character should use whatever the best thing they have is, but theoretical
analysis can help players make more informed choices in this space.

Of course, your bringing up the big picture does raise an interesting
point, which is "How much is the difference between 6 and 3 AC worth
as compared to the difference between -13 and -16?" At -13 I might very
well stick with leather armor instead of mithril no matter how early in
the game it was, unless I had no intent of casting any spells for quite
a while. Then again, if my max HP were low enough...

You're right that it's situational, of course. It's always situational.
But there are also general principles that are very useful, and "always 
shoot for a better armor class" is one of my favorites. 

>   Then there are strategies for safely changing from one suit of body
> armor to another.  1 turn to remove CoMR, 5 turns to remove body armor,
> 5 turns to wear new body armor, 1 turn to wear CoMR.  That's 10 turns
> without the CoMR, just for starters.  (Yes, it takes 5 turns for a slow
> wizard to wear or take off body armor.)

Those strategies are very similar to strategies to do things like read
spellbooks or Engrave with a hard gem safely, in my experience.

>   Most players would rather kill their characters than think about all
> this, much less do it.  In fact, most players do kill their characters
> rather than do all this.

Different people have different goals when they sit down to play a game.
Different people also have different levels of patience.

